1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,968
By the late 1800s, the world had a quiet, growing terror.

2
00:00:03,968 --> 00:00:06,983
Every harvest pulls nitrogen out of the soil,

3
00:00:06,983 --> 00:00:10,131
and nothing replaces it fast enough on its own.

4
00:00:10,131 --> 00:00:11,131
For decades,

5
00:00:11,131 --> 00:00:16,959
farmers had patched the gap with one finite resource: nitrate-rich guano and saltpeter,

6
00:00:16,959 --> 00:00:20,375
dug out of the deserts of Peru, Chile, and Bolivia.

7
00:00:20,375 --> 00:00:23,858
The scramble for it was so fierce it started a war —

8
00:00:23,858 --> 00:00:25,466
the War of the Pacific —

9
00:00:25,466 --> 00:00:28,146
and left Bolivia landlocked to this day.

10
00:00:28,146 --> 00:00:29,146
In 1898,

11
00:00:29,146 --> 00:00:34,304
the chemist William Crookes warned the British science establishment that the

12
00:00:34,304 --> 00:00:37,519
nitrate mines would run dry within a generation,

13
00:00:37,519 --> 00:00:40,064
and that mass starvation would follow.

14
00:00:40,064 --> 00:00:44,887
The only unlimited source of nitrogen was right above everyone's heads —

15
00:00:44,887 --> 00:00:45,892
the air itself.

16
00:00:45,892 --> 00:00:47,023
Nobody could unlock it.

